# Done (Milestones 1–10, security hardening, packaging, Android, iOS, dictation, chat)

## Milestone 1: Async commands

Worker thread pool for commands that would block the UI (database queries, network I/O, slow computations). Async commands receive their own arena allocator and run in parallel while the UI stays responsive.

## Milestone 2: ghostpen plugins

Global keyboard shortcuts (Wayland GlobalShortcuts portal + X11 XGrabKey), input injection (Wayland virtual-keyboard + X11 XTest, Windows SendInput), background clipboard access (Wayland data-control, X11 GdkClipboard, Windows), notifications and file dialogs.

## Milestone 3: Framework basics

Multiple windows with per-window security, window options (min/max size, fullscreen, geometry persistence), app menu bar, settings store with XDG/Known Folders support, logging to file and stderr, dev mode with Zig rebuild + restart, ReleaseSafe as the production default.

## Milestone 4: Shipping

Packaging for Linux (deb, rpm, AppImage) and development desktop entries with `.desktop` files and icon installation. Complete updater with manifest fetch, Ed25519 verification, atomic binary replacement, and in-place restart.

## Milestone 4.5: oriel CLI

The `oriel` command-line tool: `init` with React/Vue/Svelte/vanilla templates, `doctor` for environment checks, `dev`/`build`/`run`/`package`/`types`/`check` wrappers around `zig build` steps. Also `install.sh` for distribution.

## Milestone 5: ghostreel enablers

HTTP media server with range-request streaming for large video/audio files. Windows shell (`src/platform/windows/`, Win32 + WebView2 COM), all modules with Windows backends, NSIS installer with WebView2 runtime detection. Opt-in native deps: `-Dsqlite_vec` for vector search, `-Dllama` and `-Dwhisper` for local AI. CUDA GPU acceleration for llama and whisper on Linux (`-Dggml_cuda`). Audio capture (PulseAudio on Linux, live captions in ghostpen).

## Milestone 6: Windows from JavaScript

Open and manage windows from JavaScript with `oriel.window.open()`, `close()`, `setTitle()`, etc. Targeted and broadcast events. System browser via `openExternal()`. SPA fallback for client-side routers with the dot-in-last-segment caveat.

## Milestone 6.5: Oriel CLI self-update and tools

`oriel update` uses the same signed updater as Oriel apps. The CLI manages its own toolchain and dependencies without admin rights: `oriel zig` (a minisign-verified Zig manager), `oriel webview2`, `oriel setup node|nsis|all` and `oriel doctor --fix`.

## Milestone 7: macOS

AppKit + WKWebView through the Objective-C runtime. Every module has a macOS backend (tray, menu, dialogs, store, clipboard, fs_watch, global shortcuts, input, updater, audio capture), Metal for whisper/llama, and `.app` / `.dmg` packaging. Verified on macOS 15, Apple Silicon.

## Milestone 8: Deep links

Opt-in `deep_link` module: custom URL schemes open the app, or forward the link to the running instance, on Linux, Windows and macOS. Enabled in code and managed with `oriel deep-link add|register|unregister`; installers register the schemes.

## Milestone 9: Permissions

One way to declare and request OS permissions (microphone, camera, screen capture, accessibility, location, notifications, system audio) on Linux, Windows and macOS: declared once in `build.zig` (`oriel permission add`), written into each platform's package, queried and requested from Zig and JavaScript, and enforced for the webview's own requests. See [Permissions]($link.page('docs/permissions')).

## Milestone 10: Overlay windows and background apps

Driven by the GhostPen port: transparent, always-on-top, click-through windows placed on the monitor's work area (Wayland layer-shell overlays, X11, Windows, macOS), apps that start with only a tray icon, single instance with argument forwarding, `App.runOnMain`, readable command errors (`oriel.ipc.fail`), and WASAPI audio capture on Windows.

## Security hardening, part 1

Origin-bound IPC tokens (other-origin frames can't call commands), `security.freeze_prototype`, and `security.headers` (COOP, COEP, Permissions-Policy, …).

## Security hardening, part 2 (v0.6.0)

The isolation pattern (a sandboxed frame hook signs every IPC call with an HMAC key the page never sees), CSP hashes for inline scripts and styles (`strict_styles`), and macOS Developer ID signing and notarization in `oriel package`.

## Package contents (v0.6.0)

`.package.contents` chooses what every format ships besides the app: extra executables (e.g. a CLI, linked into `/usr/bin` on Linux), extra files, Oriel's runtime libraries (`libggml-cuda.so`), and stripped binaries. The Windows installer refuses to run over the running app; macOS signs nested code inside out.

## GhostPen on Oriel

The first full application built on Oriel: GhostPen, ported from Tauri, with built-in models (llama.cpp run by the app itself), live captions and dictation on Linux, Windows and macOS. Its port fixed event ordering (a command's events now reach the page before its reply, on every platform), keyboard focus for borderless windows on Windows and macOS, and added log files on macOS.

## Android

Oriel apps run on Android: a Kotlin Activity per window around `android.webkit.WebView`, driven over JNI from a Zig `liboriel.so` built with the real NDK, packaged with Gradle (APK and AAB). Verified on a Pixel phone, in Android's desktop mode and on the emulator. Modules have Android backends (AAudio, the Storage Access Framework, notifications, deep links, in-app keyboard shortcuts), and a Quick Settings tile, a foreground service and the Oriel keyboard stand in for the tray and system-wide hotkeys.

## iOS (preview)

UIKit and `WKWebView` driven from Zig through the Objective-C runtime, with no Xcode project: `.app` and `.ipa` builds, and CI runs the showcase in the iOS simulator. Not yet run on a device.

## Voice to text (`oriel.dictation`)

Live dictation and transcription with the tuning built in: whisper with voice detection, a draft model for the live text and a measured CPU-or-GPU choice per device, or the platform's own recognizer (Android). ggml is compiled optimized in every build mode and with ARM dotprod/i8mm kernels for phones. On Apple platforms the system recognizer (`SFSpeechRecognizer`) is a dictation engine too: it falls back to Apple's servers on error 300, keeps the last partial when no final arrives, and reports whether it answered from the device or from Apple's servers.

## Chat (`oriel.chat`)

A local LLM in the app, tuned the same way: models (Qwen2.5 0.5B/1.5B, Llama 3.2 1B and more), the chat template handled in Zig, streamed tokens, a reused KV cache and a mic that dictates the message. Models download once per platform (`model_download`: the release's gateway or huggingface, verified, resumed on Android via the download manager and on iOS through NSURLSession).

## One showcase

`examples/showcase` replaces the per-platform and per-feature examples: one app using every feature, for Linux, Windows, macOS, Android and iOS.

## Offline text to speech (`oriel.tts`)

Offline neural text to speech with Kokoro-82M voices: verified SHA-256 downloads, bundled espeak-ng phoneme data compiled at build time for all platforms, adaptive chunking with quick initial audio, and streaming playback on CPU or GPU (CUDA, Vulkan, Metal).

## Windows mDNS and system capabilities

Native mDNS service discovery and registration on Windows using `dnsapi`, drag-and-drop support on the iOS native renderer, and safe file dialog validation.

## Native renderer (experimental, in main)

An optional renderer for the same HTML, CSS and JavaScript, without a WebView: QuickJS, a DOM written in Zig, Yoga layout and platform drawing on Linux, Windows, macOS, Android and iOS. React, Vue, Svelte, Preact, CSS-in-JS and Alpine (with its CSP build or an explicit policy) run on it. The showcase runs in both renderers.

Implemented: incremental updates, build-time JavaScript bytecode and CSS parsing, row stamping, cached text measurements, mixed-font line boxes and baselines, inline decorations, SVG styles, native field editing, scroll events, transitions and animations. Canvas drawing works from JavaScript or Zig (`oriel.canvas`); Breakout compares both with the WebView. The render bench covers rows, animation, canvas and memory, plus power measurements on Android and Linux where readings are available. QuickJS enforces the app's CSP for dynamic code.

See [Native renderer]($link.page('docs/native-renderer')) for measured results, platform status and the supported subset of the web platform. These are development features in `main`, not a claim that every feature is in the latest tagged release.

# Next

- iOS on devices: run the showcase on an iPhone, verify Metal dictation and chat there (`zig build check -Dtarget=aarch64-ios` and the simulator CI are the base at hand).
- Native renderer: accessibility for screen readers, full CSS grid, shadow DOM and constructed style sheets; Vite live development currently uses the WebView.
- Security hardening: command scopes and local capabilities.
- Platform escape hatch: extra Info.plist keys, entitlements, Windows manifest and NSIS additions, Android Manifest snippets.
- Authenticode signing for Windows installers (`oriel signing` covers macOS self-signed certificates today).
- Windows runtime sweep: the modules marked "runtime untested on Windows" (menu accelerators, clipboard images, store, global shortcuts, input, notifications) verified on real hardware.

# Even later

- CI: `zig build test` and headless smoke checks on every push (today they run per release and for the iOS simulator)
- Per-monitor DPI scaling on Windows
- Windows tray/menu polish (hitting what "runtime untested" hides)

# How to contribute

Oriel is open source on [GitHub](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel). File issues, open pull requests, or discuss ideas there. Some guidance:

1. **Read [the contributor guide](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/blob/main/docs/contributing.md)** first for architecture and coding rules (memory safety, testing, platform coverage).
2. **Check open issues** to see what work is needed or in progress.
3. **Test Windows builds** under Wine before committing (see `docs/windows-testing.md`).
4. **Run headless checks:** `scripts/headless.sh ./zig-out/bin/oriel-smoke --auto-quit` verifies every module.
5. **Memory safety:** every PR is reviewed for leaks, null-safety, and thread safety (see `docs/memory-safety-review.md`).
